{
  "requirement": "A `{req}` opening fence MAY contain four or more colons. Habeas MUST accept opening fences of any length of at least three colons. A colon-only line with fewer colons than the opener MUST remain inside the requirement body, allowing a nested colon directive. A closing fence outside a fenced code example with at least as many colons as the opener MUST end the body.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.ci.fence_lengths`. The implementation is complete and correctly marked, and the tests cover the main cases.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `colon_fence` in `src/lib.rs` (lines 652-661) is marked `{impl}` for this requirement. It counts the leading colons and accepts any line with 3 or more. It returns the suffix and the length.\n- `definition_blocks` in `src/lib.rs` (lines 508-607) is also marked `{impl}`. It uses that length as the opener's `fence_length`, so openers of 3, 4 or 7 colons are all accepted for `{req}`.\n- The close search takes the first later line that meets three conditions. It must not overlap a Markdown code block (`overlaps_protected` against the `pulldown_cmark` `CodeBlock` ranges). It must be a colon fence with `count >= fence_length`. Its suffix must be empty after trimming.\n  - A shorter colon-only line, such as a nested `:::` inside a `::::` opener, is not a close, so it stays in the body. This allows nested directives.\n  - A longer or equal closer ends the body.\n  - A `:::` inside a fenced code example is ignored, as the requirement specifies.\n- The `{impl}` marking is complete. The only other consumer is `marker_ranges` in `src/index.rs`, which belongs to `requirements.index.chapter`. Callers such as `collect_definitions` and `preprocess_book` reach the logic through `definition_blocks`.\n- The other behavior in these functions is covered by other definitions the same code is marked for:\n  - option and anchor parsing: `references.anchors`\n  - name validation: `ci.identifiers`\n  - closed-flag and error reporting: `ci.span` and `ci.definition_errors`\n- One small unspecified detail: `colon_fence` allows up to 3 leading spaces. This follows Markdown fence convention and does not contradict the requirement. It is a minor point, not a substantive gap.\n\n**Tests** (all carry `{test}` references to this requirement)\n- `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` (`src/lib.rs` 1108-1122). A `::::{req}` block contains a nested `:::{note}…:::`. It asserts the block is closed, the body is exactly the intro plus the nested directive, and the text after the closer is outside the block.\n- `validates_four_or_more_colon_fences_and_longer_closers` (`src/evidence.rs` 1760-1793). It runs end to end through `validate_repository` with opener/closer pairs of 4/4, 4/5 and 7/8. It uses a body with a nested `:::{note}`, and requires the body to match the evidence text and the report to have no errors.\n- `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` (`src/evidence.rs` 1694-1725). A `:::` line inside a fenced code example does not end the body, and the real closer does.\n- Three-colon openers and closers are exercised throughout the existing suite.\n\nTogether these cover the boundary lengths, a longer closer, a shorter nested colon line staying in the body, and a closer inside a code example not ending the body.\n\nNo FIXME acknowledgments are needed, and none are present.",
